## Photographs and camera images

Use a photograph when it matters what a real thing looks like, such as the boiler’s reset button. Do not use stock photographs or mood pictures.

Show a camera image only where someone needs to check a place. Say which camera it is and when the picture was taken. A snapshot from 9 minutes ago is not live. [Cameras, streams and snapshots](../home-assistant/cameras.html) owns the rest.

## Keep text out of images

Put words in text, not inside a picture. Text in an image cannot be resized, copied or translated. Not every screen reader can read it, and it blurs when zoomed. Do not use a picture for a reading such as “Front door: locked”, for instructions or for a screenshot of an automation.

Text may stay in an image when it is not the point, such as a label on a boiler, or part of a household’s crest. Put anything people need, such as a model number, in text nearby. If text must sit on an image, use real HTML text or an SVG. Check its contrast against every part of the image.

## Floor plans and diagrams

Give every floor plan and diagram a short visible text description. Where it holds data, give the same facts in text.

The Picture card has an `alt_text` option. Use it for a photograph or a plan with one meaning.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/garden-plan.png
alt_text: Back garden. The shed is at the far end, with the water butt on its left.
```

The Picture elements card has no alt text option. Next to the floor plan, add a Markdown card that lists the rooms and devices that matter.

In this floor plan, each kind of sensor has its own shape as well as colour. The list under the plan gives the same facts in text.

<%= colour_example('images-floor-plan', callout: false) %>

[Charts](brand-data-charts.html#bg-dat-07) explains how to give a chart a text alternative.

## Alt text

Give every image an `alt` attribute. Write alt text for an image that carries information. Leave it empty, `alt=""`, when the text around it already says the same.

| Image | Alt text |
| --- | --- |
| A floor plan or photograph that gives information | Describe what it shows that people need |
| A decorative picture | `alt=""` |
| An icon beside a visible label | `alt=""`, because the label already says it |
| An image inside a link, where the link text names the destination | `alt=""` |

[Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) in the writing guide explains how to word alt text.

## Household identity images

A household name works on its own, with no image. If a household adds a crest or logo, keep its proportions and colours. Keep the name readable without it. [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html) explains how.
